"<strong>The loop rule as a landscape.</strong> Open the Wheatstone bridge and switch modes to the Potential view (the Potential button above the drawing), which raises every point of the circuit to its potential above the board ({#} V). Drag on the drawing to turn the view so you can see each loop. Note where the circuit climbs and where it descends, and read the node potentials. Unbalance the bridge by changing one resistor (switch back to Schematic to select it and change its value, then return to Potential) and watch how the heights of the two midpoints and the current through the galvanometer change.",
"<strong>Using the virtual apparatus.</strong> Work in the DC analysis mode with predictions and answers hidden (tick Blind: hide predictions and answers under Lab), starting from the series bulbs, parallel bulbs, voltage divider and Wheatstone bridge circuits. The bulbs here are fixed resistors. Set each part’s value, connect each ammeter in series with the part you measure and each voltmeter across it, and keep a copy of the schematic. To put an ammeter in a current path, first delete a short wire in that path and place the ammeter across the gap. To measure a voltage, connect the voltmeter beside the part with two short leads, one to each end: a part dropped on top of other parts replaces them, so a voltmeter laid straight across a bulb’s two ends takes the bulb out of the circuit. Press Record reading after each change and press Export to worksheet before you change circuits; each meter of each reading becomes a row of the Data table, with the part values. Check your own sums first, then compare them with the demo’s checks: open Kirchhoff check, press Junction rule and click a dot where parts meet, or press Loop rule and select a part.",
